            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for to suffer, then the Spanish for to turn out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: enterarse",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enterarse — \"to hear about, to find out\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Me enteré de la noticia por la radio. I heard the news on the radio."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Enterarse de algo — to find out about something. ¿Te has enterado? — have you heard?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enterarse is built on entero 'whole', inherited from Latin integer 'untouched, whole' (Lewis & Short). To find out is to get the whole story."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
